# 4. Evaluation Metrics

The following metrics were used to evaluate retrieval quality.

- **Hit Rate @ K** – Measures whether the expected article appears within the Top-K retrieved results.
- **Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)** – Measures how highly the correct result is ranked.
- **Average Manual Relevance Score (0–3)** – Measures how relevant the retrieved document is to the query.

| Score | Meaning |
|------:|---------|
| 3 | Highly Relevant |
| 2 | Relevant but Incomplete |
| 1 | Partially Relevant |
| 0 | Irrelevant |

---

# 5. Retrieval Experiments

Two retrieval experiments were conducted.

## Experiment 1 – Pure Vector Matching

Configuration:

- Embedding Model: **intfloat/multilingual-e5-base**
- Query Method: Natural language query
- Retrieval: Pure vector similarity
- Top-K: 3

### Results

| Metric | Value |
|---------|------:|
| Hit Rate | 73.3% |
| MRR | 0.66 |
| Average Relevance | 2.1 / 3.0 |

### Observation

The embedding model captured semantic meaning reasonably well, but natural language queries did not always align with the document embedding space.

---

## Experiment 2 – Template-Based Query Alignment (Final System)

Configuration:

- Embedding Model: **intfloat/multilingual-e5-base**
- Query Template:

```text
Article:
Title: {query}
Content: {query}
```

- Continuous Title Boost
- Top-K: 3

### Results

| Metric | Value |
|---------|------:|
| Hit Rate | **93.3%** |
| MRR | **0.74** |
| Average Relevance | **2.8 / 3.0** |

### Observation

Template-based query alignment significantly improved semantic retrieval by making query embeddings structurally similar to document embeddings.

